Nancy Lebovitz Wrote:
	 
	       >some people can feel that they're getting adequate input from
	       >a mere computer screen for long periods, and others don't. 
	       
With Increasing intelligence boredom will become less a problem
not more. My dog gets bored looking at the squiggles on  my
computer screen after about 100 milliseconds, even the
delightful squiggles of the Extropian list ; It takes somewhat
longer for me. Were interested in most things dogs are ,food,
sex, long walks, playing catch, praise , but we have additional
interests they can't understand. A hyper- intelligence would
undoubtedly find granger and beauty in things that are
incomprehensible or dull as dish water to us. Intelligence can
only add to our list of wonders, I don't see how it could
subtract.
 
On the other hand ,you wouldn't want to eliminate boredom
entirely. We evolved the capacity for boredom because it keeps
us from getting fixated on unproductive lines of thought. I read
somewhere ( Minsky ?) that perhaps the difference between a
genius and a normal person is that his boredom detecting
mechanism is so highly tuned that he can zero in on a solution
to a problem while we are floundering around " exploring"
endless loops.
 
This is my first post about boredom, it is not my  first boring post.
 

