From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)
Date: Thu, 8 Dec 1994 21:23:36 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-356] the Inevitability of Friedman

>>j>I don think we have a choice. Like it or not a change to
>>j>Anarcho-capitalism is inevitable. Not because somebody's
>>j>rhetoric convinces people of it's benefits and certainly not
>>j>because politicians change the law but because of technology.
>>j>Modern communication, cryptography and untraceable digital cash
>>j>will make it increasingly difficult to collect taxes.
>
Another possibility is that digital cash will prevail and tax authorities
develop only mildly intrusive agent programs to reside in smart cards and
collect taxes. 

I'm using the word "agent" in Chaum's sense, not the more common sense
applied to telecommunications and databases. 

(See _Achieving Electronic Privacy_ By David Chaum Scientific American,
August 1992.)

The agent program would have to be less intrusive than current tax
collection methods to become widely accepted. This of course would not be
hard.

As an example my smart card could have an agent program supplied by the
IRS. It would watch all my transactions throughout the year then on April
15th it would create a short message with a dollar figure and it's own
digital signature. I would transfer the money and the signature to the IRS.
That would be all the info they get from me. (After all it is their program
that figured the amount.) The program couldn't  send off my secrets because
I control the communication from my card.

My purpose here is to illustrate that anonymous digital cash doesn't
necessarily imply the end of taxes. 



