From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
Date: Thu, 8 Dec 1994 23:21:35 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-358] BASICS: Old-Growth Forestry

    I took an upper-division ecology course at San Jose State last
spring; our text and teacher asserted that one reason why redwood
forests dominate the areas they do is precisely that fire and storm do
kill off trees regularly; if not, the areas would presumably tend
toward a climax condition (in which the population densities of the
various species do not change with time), believed to be dominated by
spruce and western hemlock.  I think I remember, that the the details
of the process are that the young spruce and hemlock, which as
full-grown trees would shade the ground sufficiently to surpress new
redwood saplings, are burned off by fire often enough that few make it
to full height; I don't remember whether young redwoods simply grow
faster than spruce and hemlock, or are more fire-resistant when they
are small.
    Incidentally, the notion of "climax" is rather complex and not too
well understood; clearly, there can be biologically-driven oscillatory
equilibria as well as statically stable ones.  But possibly the most
common misunderstanding is the human tendancy to equate "old" (in
human time scale) to "stable".  'Tain't so.

    On a different subthread, I believe that naturally-occuring
forests, even climax ones, are rarely as nearly monocultures as are,
for example, tree farms.  It may be misleading to equate the two.

                        -- Jay Freeman, not the Squirrel for once...

